{
  "teamId": 120,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 Washington Nationals wrote one of the most improbable championship stories in baseball history. They stumbled out of the gate, going a combined 12-16 through March and April, buried under bullpen implosions and an identity crisis that left fans wondering whether this roster had what it took. The losses were vivid and painful — Kyle Barraclough surrendering Andrew McCutchen doubles, Sean Doolittle getting tagged repeatedly in high-leverage moments — but even in the wreckage, sparks of what was coming flickered through. Wilmer Difo homered to steal a game, Matt Adams launched clutch fly balls, and a kid named Juan Soto began announcing himself.\n\nMay was a month of wild swings, literally and figuratively. The Nationals posted a 12-17 record, the lowest point of their season, but the clips from that stretch tell a story of a team fighting with everything it had. Gerardo Parra — soon to become a fan phenomenon with his Baby Shark walk-up music — crushed a grand slam on May 11 that changed the energy in the clubhouse. Wander Suero was torched repeatedly, Doolittle continued to struggle, but Soto answered with a three-run homer on May 24 and Parra delivered a game-winning single a day earlier. The team was losing more than it was winning, yet the character being forged in these gut-punch moments would prove essential.\n\nThen something clicked. Beginning in June, the Nationals went 18-8, their best month of the year, and never looked back. They were 19-7 in August and 17-11 in September, finishing 93-69 and earning a Wild Card spot. Trea Turner delivered a grand slam in late September. Kurt Suzuki launched a three-run homer in early September that crackled with playoff electricity. The bullpen still stumbled — Daniel Hudson gave up a walk-off blast on August 30, Fernando Rodney continued to bleed — but by now the offense was overwhelming problems faster than the pitching could create them.\n\nThe postseason was the stuff of legend. Juan Soto's line drive single in the Wild Card game sent two runners home and broke Milwaukee's heart. Against the Dodgers in the Division Series, Ryan Zimmerman's three-run homer in Game 2 set the tone, Soto and Rendon kept delivering, and Washington advanced. In the World Series, the Nationals did something that had never been done in the Fall Classic — they won all four games on the road, against the Houston Astros. Anthony Rendon homered in Game 6, and Howie Kendrick — the 36-year-old journeyman who had barely survived a ruptured Achilles the previous spring — crushed a two-run shot in Game 7 that broke the game open. A team that was 19-31 in late May became World Champions, a tribute to resilience that Washington baseball fans will never forget.",
